<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For years, a recurring topic on Mac Power Users has been the travel router product category. Folks want to bring their own router with them when they go on a trip, and there have always been a lot of good reasons for it, but it&#8217;s also always been really fiddly.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I recently got the original <a href="https://store.ui.com/us/en/category/wifi-special-devices/collections/unifi-travel-router/products/utr">UniFi Travel Router</a>, and it is by far the best implementation of this product category I&#8217;ve ever seen. Setup is easy, and you can set up your own home Wi-Fi, in essence, from anywhere. It&#8217;s particularly good if you&#8217;re already invested in UniFi networking products, but you don&#8217;t need them.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">UniFi&#8217;s now followed that up with <a href="https://store.ui.com/us/en/category/wifi-special-devices/collections/unifi-travel-router/products/utr-lr">a new edition, the Long-Range model</a>, with a high-gain antenna. It still just uses Wi-Fi 5, and it&#8217;s thicker than the original model, but for Wi-Fi on the go, Wi-Fi 5 is, in my experience, just fine. Hotel networks, frankly, don&#8217;t need any more than that. If you&#8217;re looking for a travel router, this one&#8217;s worth checking out, assuming you can find it in stock.</p>
